		"brief": "An interactive demo titled \"Four numbers, one deformation\" teaching that a matrix IS a transformation of the plane, and that its columns are where the basis vectors land.\n\nCANVAS: a 2D plane centered at the origin, dark background, clean light square reference grid (spacing 1 unit), labeled axes, high-contrast sans-serif. Single self-contained HTML file, no external libraries.\n\nCONTROLS: four sliders for the entries of A = [[a, b], [c, d]], each ranging -2 to 2 in steps of 0.1, defaults a=1, b=1, c=0, d=1 (a shear). Show the matrix numerically, arranged in its 2x2 grid layout, updating live.\n\nVISUALS (all updating live and continuously while dragging a slider):\n1. The standard integer grid (all lines x = integer and y = integer) drawn transformed by A, in a faint color — so the whole plane visibly deforms.\n2. The unit square in the original plane drawn as a dashed ghost outline, and its image — the parallelogram spanned by the two columns — filled with a translucent color and outlined solid.\n3. The images of the basis vectors as bold arrows from the origin: e1 -> first column (distinct color, labeled \"col 1 = A e1\"), e2 -> second column (another color, labeled \"col 2 = A e2\"). Show the original e1 and e2 as faint dashed arrows for comparison.\n4. A draggable probe vector x, default (1.5, 0.5), drawn as a solid amber arrow from the origin. Its image Ax drawn as a bold arrow in a contrasting color. Also draw the parallelogram decomposition: the segment from the origin to x*col1, then from there to x*col1 + y*col2 = Ax, so the learner sees Ax as a weighted sum of the two columns.\n5. Readout strip: det = a*d - b*c to two decimals, with status text: if |det| > 0.02 show \"area scaled by |det|\"; if det < 0 also show \"orientation flipped\"; if |det| <= 0.02 show \"collapsed onto a line (det = 0) - the columns are parallel\" in a warning color.\n\nPRESET BUTTONS that tween the four sliders over ~0.5s: \"shear\" [[1,1],[0,1]], \"rotate 90\" [[0,-1],[1,0]], \"project onto x-axis\" [[1,0],[0,0]], \"scale 2\" [[2,0],[0,2]], \"reflect across y=x\" [[0,1],[1,0]], \"collapse\" [[1,2],[2,4]] (det = 0).\n\nREQUIREMENT: dragging any slider must never break the drawing; clamp to canvas; keep labels legible at all settings. The point that must be unmistakable within ten seconds of dragging: the two columns ARE the images of the axes, and the parallelogram is where the unit square went.",
